1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Meet the pig.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:06,000
To some, he's an ideal pet.

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:09,000
But to others, he's a four-legged meat marketer.

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
His hams, chops, bacon, and even head cheese

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
fuel a $100 billion a year business.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
We may rearrange his DNA in the name of culinary progress,

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
but this intelligent creature remains a trusted ally.

8
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:27,000
He might even save your life.

9
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, The Pig on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:46,680
The aroma of deep fried foods and the shrieks

11
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:49,520
from thrill riders say it loud and clear,

12
00:00:49,620 --> 00:00:51,520
it's time for the fair.

13
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:54,020
Surprisingly, Los Angeles, California,

14
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:57,000
hosts the largest county fair in North America.

15
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:00,000
each year attracting over 1 million visitors.

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,600
No visit is complete without paying homage

17
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:09,240
to the fair's traditional mascot, the pig.

18
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:12,340
The show pig competition is serious business,

19
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:14,220
each competitor trying to produce

20
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:17,560
the ultimate physical specimen.

21
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:21,160
Behind every champion pig is a doting mother.

22
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:23,020
This sow is a wonderful example

23
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:25,960
of what we hoped that a production sow would look like.

24
00:01:25,961 --> 00:01:26,860
would look like.

25
00:01:26,861 --> 00:01:28,600
She's 10 years old.

26
00:01:28,601 --> 00:01:31,100
She has a phenomenal udder line on her still.

27
00:01:31,101 --> 00:01:34,860
She's produced 18 litters of pigs.

28
00:01:34,861 --> 00:01:37,340
The sow is the most prolific reproducing animal

29
00:01:37,341 --> 00:01:39,240
on the farm.

30
00:01:39,241 --> 00:01:42,480
Birthing between 6 and 12 pigs per litter,

31
00:01:42,481 --> 00:01:46,340
with the capability of producing more than 250 piglets

32
00:01:46,341 --> 00:01:49,720
over her lifetime, she will spend two-thirds of her life

33
00:01:49,721 --> 00:01:51,220
pregnant.

34
00:01:51,221 --> 00:01:53,580
What you're seeing is dual activity.

35
00:01:53,581 --> 00:01:58,620
Mom is doing what she does best, and that's sleeping.

36
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:01,900
And the pigs are doing what they're doing best, and that's eating.

37
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:07,960
When they're little, within the first, oh, I'd say 12, 24 hours,

38
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:12,000
they actually lock on to one particular teat that is theirs.

39
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:14,540
And they go to it each time.

40
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:17,460
They'll fight to the death to keep that teat.

41
00:02:17,461 --> 00:02:22,240
And usually what happens is the bigger, stronger babies that are born

42
00:02:22,241 --> 00:02:28,140
root the smaller, weaker babies to the teats that don't give as much milk.

43
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:34,460
The runts sometimes succumb to the laws of natural selection and perish.

44
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:40,200
Even so, a sow is fiercely protective and will fight to the death to keep her piglets safe.

45
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:41,980
Watch her, watch her, watch her.

46
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:46,300
They're the most dangerous animal on the farm.

47
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:49,760
It's a sow with baby pigs, more so than a bull.

48
00:02:49,761 --> 00:02:53,760
in the front shoulders and the neck and the head,

49
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:55,760
comparable to a grizzly bear.

50
00:02:55,860 --> 00:02:58,760
And when they bite, they don't bite you like a dog,

51
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:01,000
sink their fangs in, and turn loose.

52
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:04,500
They bite and rip a piece of meat off of you.

53
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:10,620
So a hog bite is extremely painful, really, really serious.

54
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:14,520
A pig's powerful jaws contain 44 enamel-coated incisors,

55
00:03:14,620 --> 00:03:19,760
canines, and molars, 12 more teeth than an adult human.

56
00:03:19,761 --> 00:03:23,560
The four canines, or tusks, grow naturally,

57
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:26,560
but are often clipped off from domestic pigs days after birth

58
00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:28,400
to prevent injury to other animals.

59
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:33,540
Though well-equipped to bite, pigs

60
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:36,000
are best known for their legendary snout.

61
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:38,040
So sensitive, they are often used

62
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:41,580
to root out the highly prized edible fungi called truffles.

63
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:47,620
But most truffle hunters prefer trained dogs instead of pigs,

64
00:03:47,621 --> 00:03:50,860
who tend to eat the precious treasures.

65
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:55,700
Some call it a greedy appetite, and some call it smart.

66
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:59,360
If you're around them, you will soon figure out

67
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:04,800
that these animals have a thinking ability similar to what

68
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:06,500
people understand in the dog.

69
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:11,280
They can figure a way to tear up a fence, go through a hole.

70
00:04:11,380 --> 00:04:14,740
Terrific timekeepers.

71
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:16,380
Some have equated their intelligence

72
00:04:16,381 --> 00:04:18,880
intelligence to that of an average human 3-year-old.

73
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:25,620
But as much as we celebrate the pig's intelligence and charisma,

74
00:04:25,621 --> 00:04:28,520
we mainly enjoy eating him.

75
00:04:28,521 --> 00:04:30,820
Whether you call him a pig or a hog,

76
00:04:30,821 --> 00:04:35,740
his flesh is the world's most widely consumed meat.

77
00:04:35,741 --> 00:04:39,740
In the United States, it ranks third behind beef and chicken,

78
00:04:39,741 --> 00:04:43,040
with the average American pigging out on 51 pounds of pork

79
00:04:43,041 --> 00:04:44,040
annually.

80
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:50,120
Today's hog is a veritable butcher shop of edible meats.

81
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:53,960
The most popular products come from four distinct quarters.

82
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,020
The leg yields ham.

83
00:04:56,120 --> 00:04:59,120
The shoulder, picnic ham and roasts.

84
00:04:59,220 --> 00:05:02,000
The belly, bacon and spare ribs.

85
00:05:02,100 --> 00:05:07,260
And the loin produces tenderloins, chops, and back ribs.

86
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:08,800
Consider bacon.

87
00:05:08,900 --> 00:05:12,580
This Tyson facility processes 600,000 pounds of pork bellies

88
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:15,680
in a single day, trimming, shaping, and slicing,

89
00:05:15,681 --> 00:05:18,920
at a furious pace.

90
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:21,860
The bellies are first skinned and then injected

91
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:24,420
with a curing solution that helps preserve and flavor

92
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:26,420
the pork.

93
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:30,520
This is a critical stage in the process of making bacon.

94
00:05:30,620 --> 00:05:33,360
This is where the curing ingredients are injected

95
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:35,060
into the pork belly.

96
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:39,600
Inside this injector, there are 168 needles

97
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:43,400
that are injecting a solution of water, salt, sugar,

98
00:05:43,401 --> 00:05:45,400
sodium phosphate, sodium erythorbate,

99
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:49,400
and sodium nitrite into the belly.

100
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:54,340
A quick seasoning stop in a 135-degree thermal oven,

101
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:58,340
followed by a pressing to form rectangular blocks.

102
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:02,060
And this bacon is ready for slicing.

103
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:05,060
Little time is wasted as the slicing blade cuts the bellies

104
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:10,060
at 1,500 revolutions per minute, piecing out exact one-pound

105
00:06:10,061 --> 00:06:13,260
units that are vacuum packed and ready for sale.

106
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:19,400
Tyson even caters to bacon lovers on the go,

107
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:23,300
pre-cooked bacon.

108
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:26,680
After a high-speed slicer makes quick work of the bellies,

109
00:06:26,740 --> 00:06:30,620
the bacon slices get zapped to perfection.

110
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:33,420
This monster microwave boasts more heating power

111
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:37,360
than 2,500 home microwave ovens.

112
00:06:37,361 --> 00:06:40,600
From the time the pork bellies were put in this slicer,

113
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:44,460
cooked, chilled, and packaged, less than two minutes later,

114
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:48,480
we got a finished product ready to eat.

115
00:06:48,580 --> 00:06:51,040
If bacon is breakfast,

116
00:06:51,140 --> 00:06:55,380
a ham sandwich must be lunch.

117
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:58,180
Burgers Smokehouse in California, Missouri,

118
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:01,580
has a busy mail order business that churns out 12 million

119
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:06,060
pounds of specialty ham products every year.

120
00:07:06,061 --> 00:07:08,720
Before you can make a ham sandwich,

121
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:12,560
every whole leg brimal cut must be split and deboned.

122
00:07:12,660 --> 00:07:16,600
From here, each ham takes one of two curing directions,

123
00:07:16,700 --> 00:07:20,000
city or country.

124
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:22,300
City hams are injected with a brine mixture

125
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:27,720
for instant wet curing, the most popular way of processing ham.

126
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:31,420
Country hams are dry cured with salt and then hung up in bags

127
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:35,420
the old-fashioned way for months of curing.

128
00:07:35,421 --> 00:07:38,260
Finally, the hams meet the knife.

129
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:40,020
What we're doing is removing the femur bone

130
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:41,520
out of the center chunk of the ham.

131
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:44,660
The femur bone runs down the middle of the center.

132
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:49,100
We take our knife, and we run it around the bone

133
00:07:49,200 --> 00:07:53,500
so that we can release the bone from the rest of the ham.

134
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:58,140
Now I'm skinning the ham, removing the waste,

135
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:01,180
so when the customer gets this package of ham home,

136
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:03,740
there's very little waste.

137
00:08:03,741 --> 00:08:06,640
In the portion control room, the cutting

138
00:08:06,740 --> 00:08:08,680
gets fast and high tech.

139
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:13,080
We're cutting dry-cure country hams on band saws today.

140
00:08:13,180 --> 00:08:14,580
The work that we're doing in this room

141
00:08:14,680 --> 00:08:17,280
is primarily for the restaurant division.

142
00:08:17,380 --> 00:08:22,200
The sawers can each cut approximately 150 hams a day.

143
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:25,100
We then take the center slices that come off this product,

144
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:27,900
and then they're taken to a water knife.

145
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:29,840
The computer-controlled water knife

146
00:08:29,940 --> 00:08:33,300
applies the final trim to approximately 10,000

147
00:08:33,301 --> 00:08:37,600
pounds of ham steaks per day, far more efficient than a small

148
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:39,000
army of human trimmers.

149
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:44,740
This is the inside of the water knife.

150
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:46,640
The computer will read it.

151
00:08:46,740 --> 00:08:51,060
Then it utilizes two water jets working concurrently

152
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,160
to cut the product into specs.

153
00:08:53,260 --> 00:08:55,660
It reads both height, weight, density,

154
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:59,060
to make those calculations to be sure that the end product

155
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:02,160
is the right weight and spec.

156
00:09:02,161 --> 00:09:05,860
The jets cut with 50,000 pounds per square inch of pressure

157
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:08,700
to trim and size the steaks.

158
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:11,700
A video camera identifies and rejects slices that fall

159
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:15,600
outside of specifications.

160
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:19,520
For many ham addicts, single slices simply won't do.

161
00:09:19,620 --> 00:09:23,120
The spiral cutting process was developed a number of years

162
00:09:23,220 --> 00:09:26,460
ago, and it basically allows the consumer

163
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:30,460
to enjoy the flavor that comes from cooking the product

164
00:09:30,461 --> 00:09:34,460
with the bone in, but with the convenience of having it sliced.

165
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:37,460
The finishing touch is a sugary glaze,

166
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:41,340
caramelized by hand at 500 degrees.

167
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:44,880
Burger's ham and bacon products are packaged for sale,

168
00:09:44,980 --> 00:09:46,440
but nothing is wasted.

169
00:09:46,540 --> 00:09:50,220
What we have here are ham hocks that have just been cooked

170
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:53,680
over our slow period and cooked in water.

171
00:09:53,780 --> 00:09:57,220
We're now separating from the bone and the fat.

172
00:09:57,221 --> 00:10:01,860
the fat, we're saving the meat portion of the product.

173
00:10:01,960 --> 00:10:04,320
It's a process that has to be done by hand.

174
00:10:04,420 --> 00:10:08,960
You simply know machine will do this procedure.

175
00:10:09,060 --> 00:10:11,660
The ham hocks are mixed with a broad

176
00:10:11,760 --> 00:10:15,080
and will eventually wind up as a seasoning for turnip greens.

177
00:10:17,780 --> 00:10:20,280
These tasty hams bear little resemblance to those

178
00:10:20,380 --> 00:10:23,280
of the pig's wild ancestors.

179
00:10:23,281 --> 00:10:26,780
The earliest fossilized remains found in Africa

180
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:29,720
date back 10 million years.

181
00:10:29,820 --> 00:10:34,520
In 1539, pigs first arrived on the American mainland

182
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:36,500
when Spanish explorer Hernando de Soto

183
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:41,260
brought a small domestic herd to Tampa Bay, Florida.

184
00:10:41,360 --> 00:10:43,660
The pigs quickly became an important food source

185
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:46,440
for colonists and Native Americans alike.

186
00:10:46,540 --> 00:10:48,640
Some pigs were kept for food.

187
00:10:48,740 --> 00:10:52,100
Others were allowed to breed, and some broke loose.

188
00:10:52,101 --> 00:10:54,840
from those pigs that broke loose, that actually much

189
00:10:54,940 --> 00:10:58,540
of the pig was able to spread then throughout the colonies

190
00:10:58,640 --> 00:11:02,640
and into much of what was North America at the time.

191
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:04,680
Roaming bands of feral hogs often

192
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:08,620
used their powerful snouts to uproot crops.

193
00:11:08,720 --> 00:11:11,080
On Manhattan Island, colonial farmers

194
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:15,960
built a long, solid wall to keep out rampaging wild pigs.

195
00:11:16,060 --> 00:11:20,420
This area is today known as Wall Street.

196
00:11:20,421 --> 00:11:23,960
By the mid-1800s, Cincinnati became a central location

197
00:11:24,060 --> 00:11:26,820
for farmers delivering pigs for slaughter

198
00:11:26,920 --> 00:11:29,760
and earned the nickname Porkopolis.

199
00:11:29,860 --> 00:11:32,600
When refrigerated rail cars came along,

200
00:11:32,700 --> 00:11:36,600
it changed the whole dynamic again of how you process pigs

201
00:11:36,700 --> 00:11:39,100
and how far they could be shipped.

202
00:11:39,200 --> 00:11:41,240
Refrigeration and mechanization

203
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:45,140
revolutionized the packing houses.

204
00:11:45,240 --> 00:11:48,520
The revolution swept aside the family-owned pig farm

205
00:11:48,521 --> 00:11:53,920
and crowned the corporate farm king in the 1990s.

206
00:11:54,020 --> 00:11:57,260
The 21st century pig farm trades the old world barnyard

207
00:11:57,360 --> 00:12:00,160
for high-tech husbandry.

208
00:12:00,260 --> 00:12:05,100
This Iowa Select Farms facility is all about safety.

209
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:08,140
For the pigs, that means a mandatory shower

210
00:12:08,240 --> 00:12:11,940
and clean clothes for the workers and our camera crew.

211
00:12:12,040 --> 00:12:14,680
Biosecurity here at this farm is very important for our company

212
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:18,520
to keep outside diseases from coming into our herd.

213
00:12:18,521 --> 00:12:23,940
If we keep our pigs healthy, it increases our profitability and we have a better product for the consumer.

214
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:34,860
As we enter the facility, this is a typical wean-to-finish facility for our company.

215
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:38,280
Pigs come here at about 21 days of age.

216
00:12:38,840 --> 00:12:43,820
They will stay here until they're market ready, which is about 260 pounds.

217
00:12:43,821 --> 00:12:47,420
Once they reach that point, then they're shipped to a packer

218
00:12:47,520 --> 00:12:49,360
to be processed.

219
00:12:49,460 --> 00:12:53,760
The roll call in this facility is 2,500 pigs.

220
00:12:53,860 --> 00:12:56,760
To keep the peace, they're segregated into pens

221
00:12:56,860 --> 00:13:00,340
to reduce competition for food.

222
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:03,980
Outside, a pair of 15-ton bulk bins

223
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:08,240
supply a steady stream of pellets to each pen.

224
00:13:08,340 --> 00:13:11,980
The pigs manipulate a circular trigger to release the food.

225
00:13:11,981 --> 00:13:15,580
A precise mixture of corn, soybeans, amino acids,

226
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:16,720
and other minerals.

227
00:13:18,880 --> 00:13:22,720
Unlike past decades, when pigs were sometimes fed meat scraps,

228
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:24,520
the grain-based diet of today's pig

229
00:13:24,620 --> 00:13:28,460
is a big reason why the parasitic disease trichinosis

230
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:30,140
occurs very rarely in pork.

231
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:35,140
But what goes in must come out.

232
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:37,940
And what comes out has been plaguing farmers and the

233
00:13:38,040 --> 00:13:39,540
environment for decades.

234
00:13:39,541 --> 00:13:41,940
The way this facility is designed

235
00:13:42,040 --> 00:13:46,340
is it's a total slatted, deep pit facility.

236
00:13:46,440 --> 00:13:49,480
So the manure storage is underneath the facility.

237
00:13:49,580 --> 00:13:51,960
There's an eight-foot deep pit that

238
00:13:52,060 --> 00:13:53,860
holds the liquid manure.

239
00:13:53,960 --> 00:13:57,620
The manure is able to pass through the cracks in the floor.

240
00:13:57,720 --> 00:13:59,660
It's a slatted floor.

241
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:03,260
So the pigs have a relatively clean environment.

242
00:14:03,360 --> 00:14:06,560
The noxious pool of waste emits hydrogen sulfide,

243
00:14:06,660 --> 00:14:09,540
a toxic gas.

244
00:14:09,541 --> 00:14:12,380
Just two or three breaths of it at high concentration

245
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:14,280
can kill you.

246
00:14:14,380 --> 00:14:17,240
Approximately once a year, pumps remove the slurry

247
00:14:17,340 --> 00:14:20,020
from the subterranean tank, and farmers inject it

248
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:24,160
into their fields as fertilizer.

249
00:14:24,260 --> 00:14:26,760
But the pigs aren't too concerned about waste.

250
00:14:26,860 --> 00:14:29,560
High heat is their adversary.

251
00:14:29,660 --> 00:14:33,260
Pigs don't have sweat glands and are at risk on hot days.

252
00:14:33,360 --> 00:14:35,860
We like to keep our pigs, this size of pig,

253
00:14:35,861 --> 00:14:41,200
about 74 degrees, and we will slowly move that temperature

254
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:42,960
down as the pigs get bigger.

255
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:44,860
We want to keep the pig comfortable,

256
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:49,600
make him produce as many pounds of pork as possible.

257
00:14:49,700 --> 00:14:51,540
In this barn, temperature control

258
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:53,340
is an exact science.

259
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:56,020
When the temperature exceeds 74 degrees,

260
00:14:56,120 --> 00:14:58,920
a sensor automatically lowers a plastic curtain

261
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:02,420
to allow in the cooling breeze.

262
00:15:02,520 --> 00:15:05,420
To manage clean airflow, eight external fans

263
00:15:05,421 --> 00:15:10,220
pulled the subterranean manure gases out of the barn.

264
00:15:10,320 --> 00:15:13,220
The resulting vacuum pulls in fresh air from the attic

265
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:16,160
through vents in a continuous cycle.

266
00:15:16,260 --> 00:15:18,160
The barn can even call the farmer

267
00:15:18,260 --> 00:15:22,000
if any of the environmental controls malfunction.

268
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:24,140
While modern farmers like Iowa Select

269
00:15:24,240 --> 00:15:27,180
embrace new technology to raise a healthy pig,

270
00:15:27,280 --> 00:15:30,520
scientists are taking this a step further.

271
00:15:30,620 --> 00:15:35,380
Heart-healthy pork chops from a DNA-altered pig, anyone?

272
00:15:35,420 --> 00:15:37,420
Hey, we are the Barnyard Racers,

273
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:39,420
and we're going to have some pig racing fun

274
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:40,920
for you this afternoon.

275
00:15:41,020 --> 00:15:43,420
Hey, we're glad you're here.

276
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:46,060
The tension builds as the contestants

277
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:47,960
are loaded into the gate.

278
00:15:48,060 --> 00:15:50,760
These racing pigs may lack focus,

279
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:53,280
but their handlers know how to motivate.

280
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:55,680
They use milk and Oreo cookies.

281
00:15:55,780 --> 00:15:58,680
It's their favorite thing in the whole world.

282
00:15:58,780 --> 00:16:01,640
They may lack the supple grace of their barnyard sidekick,

283
00:16:01,740 --> 00:16:05,420
the horse, but some pigs can run out of the horse.

284
00:16:05,421 --> 00:16:07,220
mile.

285
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:09,320
Their speed is the product of breeding,

286
00:16:09,420 --> 00:16:11,820
because the modern pig is bred for a higher percentage

287
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:14,220
of lean meat to fat.

288
00:16:14,320 --> 00:16:19,760
In past centuries, a lean hog was the last thing anyone wanted.

289
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:24,180
Few pigs packed on the pounds like this 1,000 pound porker.

290
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:28,780
But one Tennessee hog named Big Bill set the world record at

291
00:16:28,880 --> 00:16:31,980
more than 2,500 pounds.

292
00:16:31,981 --> 00:16:34,780
Regrettably, no pictures of Bill exist today.

293
00:16:34,880 --> 00:16:36,820
For a farmer here 150 years ago,

294
00:16:36,920 --> 00:16:38,420
that fat was important as lard.

295
00:16:38,520 --> 00:16:39,920
They're cooking with that lard.

296
00:16:40,020 --> 00:16:41,320
They're burning lard in lamps.

297
00:16:41,420 --> 00:16:42,720
They're making soap out of it.

298
00:16:42,820 --> 00:16:44,720
So some could say that the fat was as important

299
00:16:44,820 --> 00:16:46,220
as the meat to the hog.

300
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:51,640
And so the use of the pig from 1880 to about 1940 or so

301
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:55,640
was effectively focused on production of lard.

302
00:16:55,740 --> 00:16:57,940
Lard also played a surprisingly key role

303
00:16:58,040 --> 00:16:59,640
in modern warfare.

304
00:16:59,641 --> 00:17:02,440
During World War I and World War II,

305
00:17:02,540 --> 00:17:04,720
lard was used as an essential ingredient

306
00:17:04,820 --> 00:17:08,440
in creating nitroglycerin, which is a base ingredient

307
00:17:08,540 --> 00:17:10,920
in munitions and in explosives.

308
00:17:11,020 --> 00:17:15,760
And so lard effectively was a piece of winning the war.

309
00:17:15,860 --> 00:17:19,700
By the 1950s and 60s, lard was widely replaced

310
00:17:19,800 --> 00:17:22,120
by vegetable oils as consumers began

311
00:17:22,220 --> 00:17:23,660
to avoid fattening foods.

312
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:29,260
Today's market hog has evolved from yesterday's round

313
00:17:29,261 --> 00:17:33,660
fatty lard factory with nearly three inches of back fat

314
00:17:33,760 --> 00:17:36,900
to a leaner, more muscular creature with less than one

315
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:38,700
inch of back fat.

316
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:41,180
The loin and belly are now much longer,

317
00:17:41,280 --> 00:17:44,780
ideal for producing more pounds of bacon and pork chops.

318
00:17:44,880 --> 00:17:47,780
We've also reduced the amount of calories and cholesterol

319
00:17:47,880 --> 00:17:48,740
in that animal.

320
00:17:48,840 --> 00:17:52,080
But unfortunately, the reputation of being a lard type animal,

321
00:17:52,180 --> 00:17:56,760
a fatty animal, stayed with the pig for a long time.

322
00:17:56,761 --> 00:18:01,000
So we began the Other White Meat campaign in 1986.

323
00:18:01,100 --> 00:18:03,560
Make it with the Other White Meat instead.

324
00:18:03,660 --> 00:18:06,800
The most delicious things happen when you cut into pork.

325
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:10,400
The Other White Meat.

326
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:14,120
Pork, like chicken, contains a low count of myoglobin,

327
00:18:14,220 --> 00:18:18,720
a protein that adds darker color and muscle tissue.

328
00:18:18,820 --> 00:18:21,220
The Other White Meat advertising campaign

329
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:26,220
boosted pork demand by 15% in the United States.

330
00:18:26,221 --> 00:18:29,820
The farmers help meet that demand with artificial insemination,

331
00:18:29,920 --> 00:18:32,360
or AI.

332
00:18:32,460 --> 00:18:35,460
AI increases reproductive frequency.

333
00:18:35,560 --> 00:18:38,760
More importantly, it's a perfect opportunity for pig breeders

334
00:18:38,860 --> 00:18:43,440
to select certain traits to pass along to offspring.

335
00:18:43,540 --> 00:18:46,880
International boar semen in Eldora, Iowa,

336
00:18:46,980 --> 00:18:50,880
is working to sire the finest pigs on the planet.

337
00:18:50,980 --> 00:18:54,740
Here, the genesis of our pork chops, hams, and BLTs

338
00:18:54,741 --> 00:18:56,740
is collected in small tubes.

339
00:18:56,840 --> 00:18:58,980
Artificial insemination has many benefits.

340
00:18:59,080 --> 00:19:01,480
The first is that from a cost factor,

341
00:19:01,580 --> 00:19:03,140
we can take and spread one boar out

342
00:19:03,240 --> 00:19:05,480
through very many more meetings and over much more

343
00:19:05,580 --> 00:19:07,480
of a population to spread his genetics

344
00:19:07,580 --> 00:19:08,680
throughout that population.

345
00:19:08,780 --> 00:19:11,320
It also allows greater biosecurity,

346
00:19:11,420 --> 00:19:14,100
so you have less transportation of diseases

347
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:17,100
from the boars to the sows.

348
00:19:17,200 --> 00:19:20,040
The IBS boars live the stud lifestyle

349
00:19:20,140 --> 00:19:23,540
in an isolated biosecure facility.

350
00:19:23,541 --> 00:19:26,380
Because they will sire virtually thousands of pigs,

351
00:19:26,480 --> 00:19:28,980
germ control is vital.

352
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,980
Access for the Modern Marvels camera crew

353
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:34,080
is limited to a small viewing window.

354
00:19:34,180 --> 00:19:37,020
It may not look like a female pig to you,

355
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:40,020
but this sow dummy is the only help a boar needs

356
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:41,460
to get in the mood.

357
00:19:41,560 --> 00:19:43,500
We'll use a gloved hand to make sure

358
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:47,400
that it's very sanitary to collect the boar he's extended

359
00:19:47,500 --> 00:19:50,100
out and collected, and then the semen is kept in an incubator

360
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:51,400
to maintain temperature.

361
00:19:51,401 --> 00:19:54,940
Temperature is very crucial at this point.

362
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:57,080
The natural semen has a short lifespan

363
00:19:57,180 --> 00:19:59,640
of several hours.

364
00:19:59,740 --> 00:20:02,680
But the lab technicians know how to extend that,

365
00:20:02,780 --> 00:20:06,140
and they have to be sure the semen is vital.

366
00:20:06,240 --> 00:20:11,360
We're going to get what we call a speck reading.

367
00:20:11,460 --> 00:20:14,760
That's going to help us determine the density of the semen.

368
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:20,760
We took a sample of the semen.

369
00:20:20,761 --> 00:20:22,760
And we put it on the slide here.

370
00:20:22,860 --> 00:20:26,060
We're going to look for abnormalities and motility.

371
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:30,500
As you can see, that sample looks very good.

372
00:20:30,600 --> 00:20:35,400
We don't see a lot of abnormalities in there.

373
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:37,980
Once viability is confirmed,

374
00:20:38,080 --> 00:20:41,240
the semen is added to a sugar-based extender solution

375
00:20:41,340 --> 00:20:44,480
that prolongs its integrity for several days during shipping.

376
00:20:47,780 --> 00:20:50,280
Artificial insemination helps build a system

377
00:20:50,281 --> 00:20:52,680
build a superior pig.

378
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:55,960
But researchers at the Center for Integrated Animal Genomics

379
00:20:56,060 --> 00:21:00,960
at Iowa State University want to fine tune him even further.

380
00:21:01,060 --> 00:21:03,720
They're using genetic sequencing in pursuit

381
00:21:03,820 --> 00:21:05,900
of the ultimate super swine.

382
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,960
The pig is what we might call a plastic animal.

383
00:21:09,060 --> 00:21:11,260
It's easily changed by genetics.

384
00:21:11,360 --> 00:21:12,940
This isn't by gene modification.

385
00:21:13,040 --> 00:21:16,100
This is just by selection.

386
00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:19,040
Scientists decipher the pig's genetic code

387
00:21:19,041 --> 00:21:22,640
through a complex gene sequencing process.

388
00:21:22,740 --> 00:21:26,480
We'll open up a whole new toolbox, if you like,

389
00:21:26,580 --> 00:21:30,020
of the 25,000 odd genes that the pig has.

390
00:21:30,120 --> 00:21:32,680
And we'll start to learn what they each individually do

391
00:21:32,780 --> 00:21:35,720
and what they do together to make a better pig.

392
00:21:35,820 --> 00:21:40,360
We've discovered genes that improve litter size by about 10%.

393
00:21:40,460 --> 00:21:43,000
We found genes that affect growth rate

394
00:21:43,100 --> 00:21:45,100
and reduce manure output.

395
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:48,240
We found genes that increase the amount of lean.

396
00:21:48,241 --> 00:21:50,740
And more recently, we've been working on genes that affect

397
00:21:50,840 --> 00:21:53,140
sow longevity.

398
00:21:53,240 --> 00:21:55,880
Once these genes are isolated, researchers

399
00:21:55,980 --> 00:21:58,220
can introduce changes to the gene pool

400
00:21:58,320 --> 00:22:01,860
and help pork producers cut costs and serve up a better pig.

401
00:22:04,520 --> 00:22:07,900
This pig is a clone, and he's one

402
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:11,100
of several special pigs at the University of Missouri

403
00:22:11,200 --> 00:22:14,800
that could one day make us healthier.

404
00:22:14,900 --> 00:22:16,400
These are the only pigs in the world

405
00:22:16,401 --> 00:22:21,040
that contain high levels of omega-3 fatty acids.

406
00:22:21,140 --> 00:22:26,180
Omega-3s, so-called good fats that convert to good cholesterol,

407
00:22:26,280 --> 00:22:29,680
are essential to human brain and heart function.

408
00:22:29,780 --> 00:22:32,780
But they cannot be manufactured by the human body.

409
00:22:32,880 --> 00:22:35,880
They are found only in certain plants and fish,

410
00:22:35,980 --> 00:22:38,920
like wild salmon and tuna.

411
00:22:39,020 --> 00:22:41,600
To produce a high-level omega-3 pig,

412
00:22:41,700 --> 00:22:43,900
researchers inserted a foreign cell

413
00:22:43,901 --> 00:22:47,840
containing a gene called FAT1 into fetal pig cells

414
00:22:47,940 --> 00:22:50,140
that were then cloned.

415
00:22:50,240 --> 00:22:52,100
The pig on a treadmill stress test

416
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:54,980
is one way to compare the cardiovascular system of pigs

417
00:22:55,080 --> 00:22:58,220
with high levels of omega-3s to their counterparts

418
00:22:58,320 --> 00:23:00,680
with low levels.

419
00:23:00,780 --> 00:23:04,260
The results will tell science much about how omega-3s affect

420
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:06,260
the human heart.

421
00:23:06,360 --> 00:23:07,720
I think what this will provide us

422
00:23:07,820 --> 00:23:10,800
is just another tool to really begin to understand

423
00:23:10,900 --> 00:23:12,860
the effects of omega-3 fatty acids

424
00:23:12,861 --> 00:23:15,320
on the cardiovascular system, the immune system,

425
00:23:15,420 --> 00:23:19,360
the reproductive system, the prevention of cancer.

426
00:23:19,460 --> 00:23:21,900
The medical benefits from these heart-healthy pigs

427
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,800
are potentially limitless, but pork producers

428
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:26,500
may see dollar signs.

429
00:23:26,600 --> 00:23:28,500
I look forward in the future sometime

430
00:23:28,600 --> 00:23:30,720
when FDA would approve something like this

431
00:23:30,820 --> 00:23:34,820
to enter the food chain to be able to have pork that

432
00:23:34,920 --> 00:23:38,160
has high levels of omega-3 fatty acids that would be very

433
00:23:38,260 --> 00:23:39,660
healthy to consume.

434
00:23:39,760 --> 00:23:41,860
From a walking lard factory

435
00:23:41,861 --> 00:23:45,760
to transgenic super swine, the pig has unknowingly responded

436
00:23:45,860 --> 00:23:49,100
to our attempts to rewire him.

437
00:23:49,200 --> 00:23:52,200
But sometimes, the key to producing the finest tasting

438
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:55,000
pigs on Earth is a bag of acorns.

439
00:24:00,920 --> 00:24:03,520
In South Carolina, a barbecue competition

440
00:24:03,620 --> 00:24:05,860
is right up there with the Super Bowl.

441
00:24:05,960 --> 00:24:10,320
And it's usually about one thing, slow cooked whole hog.

442
00:24:10,321 --> 00:24:12,920
This hog will cook for approximately 20 hours

443
00:24:13,020 --> 00:24:14,120
at 200 degrees.

444
00:24:14,220 --> 00:24:17,320
We like to stay low and slow because we want the meat texture

445
00:24:17,420 --> 00:24:18,220
to stay together.

446
00:24:18,320 --> 00:24:21,260
We want the pork flavor to come out.

447
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:24,440
Barbecuing low and slow will transform the toughest meat

448
00:24:24,540 --> 00:24:26,740
into a tender feast.

449
00:24:26,840 --> 00:24:29,800
But most of us don't go whole hog.

450
00:24:29,900 --> 00:24:33,840
Our pork comes cut to size and shrink wrapped.

451
00:24:33,940 --> 00:24:36,580
But as anyone who's tasted a tough chop knows,

452
00:24:36,680 --> 00:24:38,780
not all pork is created equal.

453
00:24:38,781 --> 00:24:41,580
I think consumers are becoming much more sensitive

454
00:24:41,680 --> 00:24:43,020
to food quality.

455
00:24:43,120 --> 00:24:46,520
Nobody likes to buy something that's not going to taste good.

456
00:24:46,620 --> 00:24:49,260
To be certain their meat is in peak condition,

457
00:24:49,360 --> 00:24:52,320
many producers submit samples for a pork checkup

458
00:24:52,420 --> 00:24:56,640
at Iowa State University's sensory evaluation lab.

459
00:24:56,740 --> 00:25:01,540
The lab runs the raw pork through every conceivable test.

460
00:25:01,640 --> 00:25:05,280
Still, the ultimate judge is often the human palate.

461
00:25:05,380 --> 00:25:08,080
We do trained sensory panels with tenderness,

462
00:25:08,081 --> 00:25:12,220
juiciness and flavor to actually train individuals, use them as

463
00:25:12,320 --> 00:25:16,160
instruments to determine if a producer changes genetics, we'll

464
00:25:16,260 --> 00:25:20,060
be able to tell them how it affects the quality.

465
00:25:20,160 --> 00:25:23,200
In recent years, higher quality pork is not what we once

466
00:25:23,300 --> 00:25:24,660
thought it was.

467
00:25:24,760 --> 00:25:27,140
Color is another very important factor.

468
00:25:27,240 --> 00:25:30,200
I know we've talked about the other white meat for quite some

469
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:34,180
time in relationship to nutrition, but we find that slightly darker

470
00:25:34,280 --> 00:25:36,640
pork is actually of higher quality.

471
00:25:36,641 --> 00:25:40,240
Many experts believe that darker colored, better tasting pork is

472
00:25:40,340 --> 00:25:44,540
directly related to higher fat content.

473
00:25:44,640 --> 00:25:47,680
So a few maverick farmers are seeking to return the pig to its

474
00:25:47,780 --> 00:25:51,680
more flavorful, fatty past.

475
00:25:51,780 --> 00:25:55,560
At Becker Lane, a sixth generation pig farm in Dyersville,

476
00:25:55,660 --> 00:26:00,760
Iowa, this throwback approach is called organic pig farming,

477
00:26:00,860 --> 00:26:03,600
a way of life that might cause the pigs at a large corporate

478
00:26:03,601 --> 00:26:06,500
to turn green with envy.

479
00:26:06,600 --> 00:26:10,900
Our emphasis here is providing quality of life for the pig

480
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:14,240
and also for the farmers that are involved in the production.

481
00:26:14,340 --> 00:26:16,940
So we provide the pig with a lot more space.

482
00:26:17,040 --> 00:26:20,080
We believe that the pigs, when they are given so much space

483
00:26:20,180 --> 00:26:23,560
to move around and eat organic grain,

484
00:26:23,660 --> 00:26:28,020
that will show up and express itself in the texture of the meat.

485
00:26:28,120 --> 00:26:30,720
The Becker Lane pigs begin their free-range life

486
00:26:30,820 --> 00:26:32,960
in a farrowing field.

487
00:26:32,961 --> 00:26:36,560
A place where sows give birth and nurture their piglets.

488
00:26:36,660 --> 00:26:40,440
So every sow has her own space.

489
00:26:40,540 --> 00:26:45,700
So she can have her own food and her own house to live in,

490
00:26:45,800 --> 00:26:47,580
which we call farrowing hut.

491
00:26:47,680 --> 00:26:50,740
She's free to go in there and give birth to her piglets

492
00:26:50,840 --> 00:26:55,080
and come out here and drink from this water system.

493
00:26:55,180 --> 00:26:57,020
If she wants, she can make a mud hole.

494
00:26:57,120 --> 00:26:59,420
So they have lots of options.

495
00:26:59,520 --> 00:27:02,960
They can live a normal pig life.

496
00:27:02,961 --> 00:27:04,800
Thanks to their unaltered genetics,

497
00:27:04,900 --> 00:27:07,060
the pigs retain extra fat layers.

498
00:27:07,160 --> 00:27:09,600
And with the help of insulated farrowing huts,

499
00:27:09,700 --> 00:27:14,200
they can withstand the harshest Midwest winters.

500
00:27:14,300 --> 00:27:16,140
When the piglets reach seven weeks,

501
00:27:16,240 --> 00:27:19,280
they move to some European-style accommodations,

502
00:27:19,380 --> 00:27:21,120
the hoop barn.

503
00:27:21,220 --> 00:27:23,320
So this is a hoop barn.

504
00:27:23,420 --> 00:27:30,460
And inside, you see we have some pigs.

505
00:27:30,461 --> 00:27:35,940
And a hoop barn is any building that we is built with these

506
00:27:36,040 --> 00:27:39,040
metal poles to create a rafter system.

507
00:27:39,140 --> 00:27:42,340
And over the metal poles, we put some kind of fabric,

508
00:27:42,440 --> 00:27:43,540
in this case, plastic.

509
00:27:43,640 --> 00:27:45,800
We're trying to manipulate the microclimate here

510
00:27:45,900 --> 00:27:50,240
so the pigs can be comfortable all the time.

511
00:27:50,340 --> 00:27:52,380
And that reduces stress.

512
00:27:52,480 --> 00:27:56,020
So how do these pigs stay warm when it's freezing?

513
00:27:56,120 --> 00:27:58,580
It's all in the straw.

514
00:27:58,581 --> 00:28:01,320
Originally invented in Sweden, a hoop barn

515
00:28:01,420 --> 00:28:04,680
uses a two-foot-deep layer of straw that absorbs

516
00:28:04,780 --> 00:28:09,120
and decomposes the pig's manure, generating heat.

517
00:28:09,220 --> 00:28:11,520
The mixture is replaced regularly

518
00:28:11,620 --> 00:28:14,220
and composted on the farm.

519
00:28:14,320 --> 00:28:18,040
This eco-recycling is responsible farm management,

520
00:28:18,140 --> 00:28:21,940
but it's the pedigreed pigs that bring home the bacon.

521
00:28:22,040 --> 00:28:25,880
Becker Lane specializes in purebred heritage breeds,

522
00:28:25,881 --> 00:28:29,320
such as the jet black Berkshire hog.

523
00:28:29,420 --> 00:28:32,320
The Berkshires fame began in the 17th century,

524
00:28:32,420 --> 00:28:34,920
when Oliver Cromwell's army brought a small herd back

525
00:28:35,020 --> 00:28:38,320
to the English royal family.

526
00:28:38,420 --> 00:28:43,100
The king later gave a gift of Berkshire hogs to the Japanese.

527
00:28:43,200 --> 00:28:45,160
Today, it's reverently known in Japan

528
00:28:45,260 --> 00:28:48,240
as kuributa, or black pig.

529
00:28:48,340 --> 00:28:50,280
And the vast majority of American-raised Berkshire

530
00:28:50,380 --> 00:28:54,280
pork is exported to Japan.

531
00:28:54,281 --> 00:28:56,680
In the United States, it's now a trendy main course

532
00:28:56,780 --> 00:28:58,580
in upscale restaurants.

533
00:28:58,680 --> 00:29:01,180
The pure Berkshire pork, or kurabuta,

534
00:29:01,280 --> 00:29:03,080
is definitely darker.

535
00:29:03,180 --> 00:29:08,720
You'll see a darker wine color to the pork and more marbling.

536
00:29:08,820 --> 00:29:10,720
And marbling is intramuscular fat.

537
00:29:10,820 --> 00:29:12,940
That's the obvious difference.

538
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:17,340
And when you taste it, there's a lot of conclusive data

539
00:29:17,440 --> 00:29:21,380
that shows the difference in taste for that pork.

540
00:29:21,480 --> 00:29:23,240
Becker's latest Old World experiment

541
00:29:23,241 --> 00:29:27,340
is a bit nutty, specifically acorns

542
00:29:27,440 --> 00:29:30,140
for the famous Italian ham prosciutto.

543
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:32,680
The tradition of feeding acorns to pigs

544
00:29:32,780 --> 00:29:36,180
goes back through medieval times.

545
00:29:36,280 --> 00:29:39,660
And we have recipes as far back as Caesar campaigning in Gaul

546
00:29:39,760 --> 00:29:42,600
and his troops carrying prosciutto that's

547
00:29:42,700 --> 00:29:44,860
been finished with acorns.

548
00:29:44,960 --> 00:29:47,000
The rear legs of these acorn-noshing pigs

549
00:29:47,100 --> 00:29:50,400
will soon be transformed into upscale prosciutto ham,

550
00:29:50,401 --> 00:29:55,700
downstate at La Quertia in Norwalk, Iowa.

551
00:29:55,800 --> 00:29:58,540
La Quertia is one of a handful of artisan American prosciutto

552
00:29:58,640 --> 00:30:00,840
makers.

553
00:30:00,940 --> 00:30:04,140
The first step in curing it is to remove moisture.

554
00:30:04,240 --> 00:30:06,840
We apply salt externally.

555
00:30:06,940 --> 00:30:08,060
We don't tumble it.

556
00:30:08,160 --> 00:30:09,460
We don't inject it.

557
00:30:09,560 --> 00:30:10,860
We don't cook it.

558
00:30:10,960 --> 00:30:12,560
We don't spice it.

559
00:30:12,660 --> 00:30:14,560
We don't ferment it.

560
00:30:14,660 --> 00:30:17,860
We just put the salt on, and then we use movement

561
00:30:17,861 --> 00:30:22,600
temperatures to allow the salt to penetrate and the flavor

562
00:30:22,700 --> 00:30:23,860
to develop in the ham.

563
00:30:23,960 --> 00:30:26,100
We use the refrigeration to take the moisture

564
00:30:26,200 --> 00:30:27,700
out of the meat.

565
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:30,300
We move it from a very rapid drying situation

566
00:30:30,400 --> 00:30:33,800
to a much slower drying situation.

567
00:30:33,900 --> 00:30:36,220
In the centuries prior to refrigeration,

568
00:30:36,320 --> 00:30:39,220
prosciutto makers used the changing seasons to preserve

569
00:30:39,320 --> 00:30:43,560
and then dry cure the ham by slaughtering and salting

570
00:30:43,660 --> 00:30:46,260
in the frigid winter and then drying and aging

571
00:30:46,261 --> 00:30:49,200
through the spring and summer.

572
00:30:49,300 --> 00:30:50,900
When the salt is applied, the cold

573
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:54,160
inhibits bacterial activity, giving time for the salt

574
00:30:54,260 --> 00:30:57,800
to fully penetrate and push out moisture.

575
00:30:57,900 --> 00:31:00,200
The salt protects the meat from bacteria

576
00:31:00,300 --> 00:31:02,980
as it slowly dries, shrinking up to 2

577
00:31:03,080 --> 00:31:04,320
thirds of its original size.

578
00:31:06,920 --> 00:31:09,520
After approximately 10 months of aging,

579
00:31:09,620 --> 00:31:14,020
the hams are trimmed, packaged, and shipped.

580
00:31:14,021 --> 00:31:16,480
The price is as high as $30 a pound.

581
00:31:16,580 --> 00:31:20,660
It's a gourmet delight for discerning ham lovers in the know.

582
00:31:20,760 --> 00:31:25,800
I always like to say the British mariners ate salt pork

583
00:31:25,900 --> 00:31:28,640
and the Roman legionnaires ate prosciutto.

584
00:31:28,740 --> 00:31:31,400
You know, who do you want to be?

585
00:31:31,500 --> 00:31:33,740
With little thought, we take this versatile animal's

586
00:31:33,840 --> 00:31:38,380
life to concoct countless mouthwatering edibles.

587
00:31:38,480 --> 00:31:41,040
But one day, when you least expect it,

588
00:31:41,041 --> 00:31:43,880
The pig may just save your life.

589
00:31:47,880 --> 00:31:49,880
Every day, millions of people worldwide

590
00:31:49,980 --> 00:31:53,880
count on the pig as an important source of protein.

591
00:31:53,980 --> 00:31:57,080
But along with feeding us, he's saving our lives in ways

592
00:31:57,180 --> 00:32:00,100
few could ever have imagined.

593
00:32:00,200 --> 00:32:02,900
We're short of human organ donors.

594
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:07,500
But today, science holds great hope for xenotransplantation,

595
00:32:07,600 --> 00:32:10,300
or the process of taking an organ from an animal

596
00:32:10,301 --> 00:32:13,340
transplanting it into a human.

597
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:16,780
Presently, science is counting on the pig, an animal whose

598
00:32:16,880 --> 00:32:20,140
organs and circulatory system are strikingly similar to a

599
00:32:20,240 --> 00:32:21,840
human's.

600
00:32:21,940 --> 00:32:24,580
The advantage of pigs is that their physiology is actually

601
00:32:24,680 --> 00:32:26,880
pretty close to humans, surprisingly.

602
00:32:26,980 --> 00:32:29,820
For example, insulin, pig insulin was used before human

603
00:32:29,920 --> 00:32:32,720
insulin was available for diabetics.

604
00:32:32,820 --> 00:32:36,160
And it differs by just a single amino acid from the human

605
00:32:36,260 --> 00:32:37,920
insulin.

606
00:32:38,020 --> 00:32:40,300
While medical science still has a long way

607
00:32:40,301 --> 00:32:42,940
before doctors can routinely transplant pig organs

608
00:32:43,040 --> 00:32:44,940
into humans.

609
00:32:45,040 --> 00:32:47,400
It's much closer to developing a life-saving use

610
00:32:47,500 --> 00:32:50,940
for certain pig cells.

611
00:32:51,040 --> 00:32:54,420
Approximately 1 million people with type 1 diabetes

612
00:32:54,520 --> 00:32:57,420
lack pancreatic islet cells that the body needs

613
00:32:57,520 --> 00:33:00,480
to metabolize sugar.

614
00:33:00,580 --> 00:33:04,520
Without these cells, long-term complications like kidney disease

615
00:33:04,620 --> 00:33:07,220
and blindness can occur.

616
00:33:07,221 --> 00:33:09,720
At Chimerics in Blair, Nebraska,

617
00:33:09,820 --> 00:33:11,960
the race is on to perfect a way to transfer

618
00:33:12,060 --> 00:33:14,960
these pancreatic cells from pig to human

619
00:33:15,060 --> 00:33:18,260
and forever end insulin dependence.

620
00:33:18,360 --> 00:33:21,460
This is a pancreas from a pig that we removed

621
00:33:21,560 --> 00:33:23,360
a couple hours ago.

622
00:33:23,460 --> 00:33:26,740
And what we plan to do today is to isolate

623
00:33:26,840 --> 00:33:28,980
the pancreatic islet cell.

624
00:33:29,080 --> 00:33:31,220
And this is what we hope to eventually transplant

625
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:35,680
into patients with diabetes to allow them to metabolize

626
00:33:35,780 --> 00:33:37,220
their glucose better.

627
00:33:37,221 --> 00:33:39,920
The challenge is to perfect a method to transplant

628
00:33:40,020 --> 00:33:43,460
the pig's islet cells so that a human's white blood cells

629
00:33:43,560 --> 00:33:46,960
won't destroy the foreign pig cells.

630
00:33:47,060 --> 00:33:49,960
Scientists cut the pancreas into fine pieces

631
00:33:50,060 --> 00:33:52,460
and isolate the islets.

632
00:33:52,560 --> 00:33:56,880
Later, they'll be cryopreserved until needed.

633
00:33:56,980 --> 00:33:58,980
When clinical testing is completed,

634
00:33:59,080 --> 00:34:02,420
and if a procedure is proven safe and effective for humans,

635
00:34:02,520 --> 00:34:07,220
type 1 diabetics may have a cure within the next decade.

636
00:34:07,221 --> 00:34:11,020
While diabetics play the waiting game,

637
00:34:11,120 --> 00:34:13,820
some 80,000 Americans already know the pig

638
00:34:13,920 --> 00:34:16,260
can mend a broken heart.

639
00:34:16,360 --> 00:34:18,740
Sterilized and molded porcine heart valves

640
00:34:18,840 --> 00:34:22,460
are frequent replacements for faulty human valves.

641
00:34:22,560 --> 00:34:26,040
The human aortic heart valve opens and closes about 70 times

642
00:34:26,140 --> 00:34:29,380
per minute in an adult male, regulating blood flow

643
00:34:29,480 --> 00:34:32,040
as it is pumped from the heart to the rest of the body.

644
00:34:32,041 --> 00:34:34,700
of the body.

645
00:34:34,800 --> 00:34:38,340
Surgeons implant a porcine valve if the human valve contains

646
00:34:38,440 --> 00:34:43,580
a defect or simply wears out with age.

647
00:34:43,680 --> 00:34:48,020
The pig's valve can last more than 25 years, one reason why

648
00:34:48,120 --> 00:34:54,500
more than 500,000 people worldwide have porcine valves.

649
00:34:54,600 --> 00:34:57,040
Compared to other animals, the pig's aortic valve

650
00:34:57,140 --> 00:34:59,940
is amazingly durable.

651
00:34:59,941 --> 00:35:02,580
The same goes for his cast iron intestine,

652
00:35:02,680 --> 00:35:05,620
a fact that is turning millions of people with severe injuries

653
00:35:05,720 --> 00:35:08,680
into big fans of the pig.

654
00:35:08,780 --> 00:35:10,880
In this case, it's kind of a pig patch kit.

655
00:35:14,180 --> 00:35:16,860
Cook Biotech in West Lafayette, Indiana,

656
00:35:16,960 --> 00:35:21,160
manufactures a product called Sergisys Biodesign,

657
00:35:21,260 --> 00:35:24,700
taken directly from a pig's small intestine submucosa,

658
00:35:24,800 --> 00:35:26,840
a thin layer of strong connective tissue.

659
00:35:26,841 --> 00:35:31,840
Once sterilized and processed, it's placed directly onto human

660
00:35:31,940 --> 00:35:35,320
tissue, where it can help heal dozens of ailments,

661
00:35:35,420 --> 00:35:38,860
from severe wounds and burns to hernias,

662
00:35:38,960 --> 00:35:42,160
and tissue repairs in formerly conjoined twins.

663
00:35:42,260 --> 00:35:45,420
For a long time, surgeons have been looking for ways

664
00:35:45,520 --> 00:35:48,260
to repair the body's soft tissue.

665
00:35:48,360 --> 00:35:50,560
And in many cases, they've put metals,

666
00:35:50,660 --> 00:35:54,540
and they've put plastics, and even more exotic composite

667
00:35:54,541 --> 00:35:56,840
composite and polymer materials.

668
00:35:56,940 --> 00:35:58,480
But what they really wanted to do

669
00:35:58,580 --> 00:36:02,840
was put a biologic material in that would actually respond

670
00:36:02,940 --> 00:36:07,880
to the body and, over time, allow the body to heal itself.

671
00:36:07,980 --> 00:36:10,880
The biodesign acts as a three-dimensional matrix,

672
00:36:10,980 --> 00:36:15,100
or a scaffold, for the human tissue to remodel itself.

673
00:36:15,200 --> 00:36:18,600
When implanted, the human tissues deliver cells and nutrients,

674
00:36:18,700 --> 00:36:23,100
encouraging capillaries to grow and rebuild the damaged site.

675
00:36:23,101 --> 00:36:26,300
Then after it has been used, it actually goes away,

676
00:36:26,400 --> 00:36:27,600
which is nice.

677
00:36:27,700 --> 00:36:30,640
And now you leave behind a nice, strong repair

678
00:36:30,740 --> 00:36:33,440
that's truly human tissue.

679
00:36:33,540 --> 00:36:36,340
Inside the Cook Biotech labs, technicians

680
00:36:36,440 --> 00:36:38,340
cut and fashion the biodesign material

681
00:36:38,440 --> 00:36:41,460
in many shapes and sizes, depending on the geometry

682
00:36:41,560 --> 00:36:42,960
and purpose of the patient's need.

683
00:36:46,660 --> 00:36:50,760
Cook also inspects the material to catch imperfections.

684
00:36:50,860 --> 00:36:52,860
Before it's approved for use, technicians

685
00:36:52,861 --> 00:36:56,000
run a battery of tests, including one for durability

686
00:36:56,100 --> 00:36:58,660
that repeatedly stretches the material,

687
00:36:58,760 --> 00:37:01,600
simulating the repetitive physical forces in the human body.

688
00:37:05,100 --> 00:37:07,200
When all quality checks are completed,

689
00:37:07,300 --> 00:37:09,620
technicians heat seal the biodesign material

690
00:37:09,720 --> 00:37:13,880
in sterile packages and distribute it throughout the world.

691
00:37:13,980 --> 00:37:16,980
A pig patch kit with the power to jumpstart healing

692
00:37:17,080 --> 00:37:19,760
in virtually all regions of the human body.

693
00:37:19,860 --> 00:37:22,620
I think in the future, the pig will be a source

694
00:37:22,621 --> 00:37:24,660
of different matrix materials.

695
00:37:24,760 --> 00:37:27,220
It also could lead to the delivery of drugs,

696
00:37:27,320 --> 00:37:30,560
the ability to repair tissues all over the body,

697
00:37:30,660 --> 00:37:34,760
even tissues that we haven't even thought of repairing now.

698
00:37:34,860 --> 00:37:36,760
Although the pig's future in medicine

699
00:37:36,860 --> 00:37:39,940
offers great promise, some aren't as interested

700
00:37:40,040 --> 00:37:43,440
in transplanting its organs as much as they are in sauteing

701
00:37:43,540 --> 00:37:46,480
them with mushrooms, capers, and lemon.

702
00:37:46,481 --> 00:37:47,480
and lemon.

703
00:37:50,860 --> 00:37:53,020
When we look at the pig, most of us

704
00:37:53,120 --> 00:37:57,020
see bacon for breakfast and pork chops for dinner.

705
00:37:57,120 --> 00:38:03,160
It may be hard to also visualize tires, cosmetics, glue, shoes,

706
00:38:03,260 --> 00:38:05,660
and hundreds of other products.

707
00:38:05,760 --> 00:38:08,800
Yet in each, nearly one third of our porcine pal

708
00:38:08,900 --> 00:38:11,740
is a basic ingredient.

709
00:38:11,840 --> 00:38:15,380
Fat becomes lard and may find its way into lipstick, paint,

710
00:38:15,381 --> 00:38:17,380
or crayons.

711
00:38:17,480 --> 00:38:19,820
Scrap tissue is reduced to protein meal

712
00:38:19,920 --> 00:38:23,120
and may become pet food or chicken feed.

713
00:38:23,220 --> 00:38:26,320
Bone, when cooked down, excretes gelatin

714
00:38:26,420 --> 00:38:29,420
that helps produce x-rays and film.

715
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:32,640
Even the organs and tidbits, like the tongue and the feet,

716
00:38:32,740 --> 00:38:35,800
collectively known as awful, are a prized food source

717
00:38:35,900 --> 00:38:38,240
in Asia, Europe, and other continents.

718
00:38:38,340 --> 00:38:40,640
We don't use a lot of the awful products.

719
00:38:40,740 --> 00:38:43,540
And we are able to ship them to countries that do utilize them

720
00:38:43,640 --> 00:38:45,080
and are willing to pay for them.

721
00:38:45,081 --> 00:38:47,780
And it makes up a fairly substantial percentage

722
00:38:47,880 --> 00:38:52,120
of the actual profit for the animals that are being shipped.

723
00:38:52,220 --> 00:38:54,620
A handful of trailblazing chefs in America

724
00:38:54,720 --> 00:38:57,560
are trying to serve up odd pig parts to the rest of us.

725
00:38:59,700 --> 00:39:02,440
At Encanto Restaurant in San Francisco,

726
00:39:02,540 --> 00:39:07,400
the house specialty is awful, like tongue, brain, heart,

727
00:39:07,500 --> 00:39:12,680
liver, kidney, feet, and intestine, or chitlings.

728
00:39:12,780 --> 00:39:14,640
Call it Back to the Future.

729
00:39:14,641 --> 00:39:18,120
My grandparents' generation was a firm believer in using it

730
00:39:18,121 --> 00:39:20,300
because they were of the World War II generation.

731
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:22,300
You know, they had the rationing stamps,

732
00:39:22,301 --> 00:39:25,440
and they could go further with buying a tongue

733
00:39:25,441 --> 00:39:28,420
than they could with a pork chop or a pork loin.

734
00:39:29,400 --> 00:39:32,260
In the Encanto kitchen, it all starts from the top.

735
00:39:32,900 --> 00:39:35,180
What we're going to do is debone this pig's head

736
00:39:35,181 --> 00:39:36,760
and set it up for porchetta detesta,

737
00:39:37,060 --> 00:39:39,980
and then I will remove the brain from the skull.

738
00:39:40,740 --> 00:39:43,160
It takes just a little patience to debone a pig's head.

739
00:39:43,161 --> 00:39:49,000
It's not that labor intensive if you're accustomed to it.

740
00:39:49,001 --> 00:39:52,840
I got a good crack in there now, so she's going to.

741
00:39:52,841 --> 00:39:55,400
There she goes.

742
00:39:55,401 --> 00:39:56,640
What did you do yesterday, honey?

743
00:39:56,641 --> 00:39:58,000
Oh, no, nothing big.

744
00:39:58,001 --> 00:39:59,840
I just split a pig's head in half.

745
00:39:59,841 --> 00:40:03,140
The brain is too soft and tender to actually sear.

746
00:40:03,141 --> 00:40:04,220
So we're going to poach it.

747
00:40:04,221 --> 00:40:06,760
You want a nice gentle poach.

748
00:40:06,761 --> 00:40:07,461
OK?

749
00:40:07,462 --> 00:40:08,380
So I'm just going to take that brain.

750
00:40:08,381 --> 00:40:09,180
I'm not seasoning it.

751
00:40:09,181 --> 00:40:12,420
I'm just going to drop it right in, ever so gently.

752
00:40:12,421 --> 00:40:15,420
So, brain's been poached ever so delicately.

753
00:40:15,421 --> 00:40:18,260
I'm gonna let that sit there and cool.

754
00:40:18,261 --> 00:40:20,220
So now it's nice and cool, okay?

755
00:40:20,221 --> 00:40:22,760
So I'm gonna season that a little salt.

756
00:40:22,761 --> 00:40:23,760
Fresh ground pepper.

757
00:40:23,761 --> 00:40:26,060
The nuttiness and earthiness of the mushrooms

758
00:40:26,061 --> 00:40:29,100
will really contrast very well with the brain.

759
00:40:29,101 --> 00:40:32,900
So, I'm gonna add a piece of butter,

760
00:40:32,901 --> 00:40:34,380
and now I'm gonna add the brain.

761
00:40:34,381 --> 00:40:37,380
Right where that butter is, it's gonna help caramelize,

762
00:40:37,381 --> 00:40:39,620
give a nice nutty flavor to it, okay?

763
00:40:39,621 --> 00:40:41,480
Eating brains will not make you smarter.

764
00:40:41,481 --> 00:40:43,820
I've actually been asked that a couple times.

765
00:40:43,920 --> 00:40:44,980
Are we going to be smarter today?

766
00:40:45,080 --> 00:40:46,020
No, not really.

767
00:40:46,120 --> 00:40:47,620
We're going to come right over here.

768
00:40:47,720 --> 00:40:49,320
See that brain right there?

769
00:40:49,420 --> 00:40:51,960
So we have these beautiful chanterelle mushrooms

770
00:40:52,060 --> 00:40:55,720
with capers and lemon brown butter.

771
00:40:55,820 --> 00:41:03,100
And the piste resistance is your fresh pork brain right there.

772
00:41:03,200 --> 00:41:05,800
We'll finish that off with a little bit of its sauce.

773
00:41:08,500 --> 00:41:11,480
It may not be brains like your grandfather do them.

774
00:41:11,481 --> 00:41:13,680
But over the last two centuries, many Americans

775
00:41:13,780 --> 00:41:17,380
were accustomed to pitching in on slaughtering day.

776
00:41:17,480 --> 00:41:20,020
Then you would have all the fall cuts would be turned

777
00:41:20,120 --> 00:41:22,520
into something like a pâtés or terrines.

778
00:41:22,620 --> 00:41:23,720
Sausages would be made.

779
00:41:23,820 --> 00:41:27,220
Legs would be turned into hams, city hams and salt-cured hams.

780
00:41:27,320 --> 00:41:29,040
And the head would be made into head cheese.

781
00:41:29,140 --> 00:41:32,240
This was a normal way of life in this country just as much

782
00:41:32,340 --> 00:41:35,140
as it was all throughout Europe.

783
00:41:35,240 --> 00:41:38,140
Encanto has evolved the family farm ritual

784
00:41:38,240 --> 00:41:40,140
into haute cuisine.

785
00:41:40,141 --> 00:41:42,140
How about some kidney with lentils?

786
00:41:42,240 --> 00:41:45,140
Or trotter cakes, a new spin on pigs' feet?

787
00:41:45,240 --> 00:41:46,840
Save room for dessert.

788
00:41:46,940 --> 00:41:50,880
So what we've done is we've taken this great pork blood

789
00:41:50,980 --> 00:41:54,180
here, and we've made a final dessert.

790
00:41:54,280 --> 00:41:57,400
And it's a chocolate blood panna cotta with ancient spice,

791
00:41:57,500 --> 00:41:59,600
which is poivlong and cinnamon and cloves,

792
00:41:59,700 --> 00:42:02,200
so almost like your Christmassy spices.

793
00:42:02,300 --> 00:42:05,500
Blood was a very traditional thickener for sauces and desserts

794
00:42:05,600 --> 00:42:06,940
back in the day.

795
00:42:07,040 --> 00:42:08,300
And this one is always a big surprise.

796
00:42:08,400 --> 00:42:10,140
You would never know there's blood in there ever.

797
00:42:10,141 --> 00:42:12,540
unless I told you.

798
00:42:12,541 --> 00:42:15,180
Encanto serves a monthly head-to-tail dinner

799
00:42:15,181 --> 00:42:16,740
that's a regular sellout.

800
00:42:16,741 --> 00:42:18,940
There's the direct correlation to oneself, where they say,

801
00:42:18,941 --> 00:42:20,180
oh, I can't eat tongue, I have one,

802
00:42:20,181 --> 00:42:21,820
or I can't eat heart, I have one.

803
00:42:21,821 --> 00:42:24,460
So it's getting people to just be willing to try it

804
00:42:24,461 --> 00:42:25,460
for the first time.

805
00:42:25,461 --> 00:42:28,520
And I think that's when you change their opinion.

806
00:42:28,521 --> 00:42:31,300
Most of us may not be serving up that blood and chocolate

807
00:42:31,301 --> 00:42:33,800
panna cotta anytime soon.

808
00:42:33,801 --> 00:42:35,940
But there's little doubt that the pig will be on our dinner

809
00:42:35,941 --> 00:42:39,600
tables for years to come.

810
00:42:39,601 --> 00:42:42,700
Though technology has transformed him into a walking meat factory,

811
00:42:43,400 --> 00:42:46,460
and science has enlisted him to heal our ailments,

812
00:42:47,400 --> 00:42:50,980
the pig will always have his exclusive role in the barnyard

813
00:42:50,981 --> 00:42:55,160
and the distinction as the smartest critter on the farm.

